1
00:00:00,250 --> 00:00:03,007
On the twentieth of July,
nineteen sixty

2
00:00:03,017 --> 00:00:03,820
nine,

3
00:00:03,830 --> 00:00:07,320
more than half a billion people
around the world crowded around

4
00:00:07,330 --> 00:00:08,350
television sets.

5
00:00:09,333 --> 00:00:11,343
They watched a fuzzy black and
white picture,

6
00:00:11,353 --> 00:00:14,503
beamed from almost four hundred
thousand kilometres

7
00:00:14,513 --> 00:00:15,313
away.

8
00:00:16,000 --> 00:00:18,530
A man in a white suit climbed
down a ladder,

9
00:00:18,540 --> 00:00:19,980
and stepped onto the Moon.

10
00:00:20,833 --> 00:00:23,803
It was the end of a race between
two superpowers,

11
00:00:23,813 --> 00:00:27,263
that began with a beeping metal
ball, just twelve

12
00:00:27,273 --> 00:00:28,073
years earlier.

13
00:00:29,733 --> 00:00:32,983
After the Second World War, the
United States and the

14
00:00:32,993 --> 00:00:35,713
Soviet Union became rivals in
the Cold War.

15
00:00:36,417 --> 00:00:39,027
Both had captured German rocket
technology,

16
00:00:39,037 --> 00:00:42,537
and rocket scientists, at the
end of the war.

17
00:00:43,250 --> 00:00:46,620
Rockets that could carry nuclear
weapons across the world

18
00:00:46,630 --> 00:00:49,110
could also carry satellites into
orbit.

19
00:00:49,833 --> 00:00:53,143
On the fourth of October,
nineteen fifty seven,

20
00:00:53,153 --> 00:00:56,463
the Soviet Union launched
Sputnik, a polished metal

21
00:00:56,473 --> 00:00:58,493
ball about the size of a beach
ball.

22
00:00:59,250 --> 00:01:00,630
It did nothing but beep.

23
00:01:00,810 --> 00:01:03,890
But anyone with a radio could
hear it passing overhead,

24
00:01:04,470 --> 00:01:05,710
every ninety six minutes.

25
00:01:06,583 --> 00:01:09,760
Its batteries died after three
weeks, and in January nineteen

26
00:01:09,770 --> 00:01:12,913
fifty eight, it fell back and
burned up in the

27
00:01:12,923 --> 00:01:13,723
atmosphere.

28
00:01:14,023 --> 00:01:15,083
But the world had changed.

29
00:01:16,000 --> 00:01:17,350
Americans were shocked.

30
00:01:17,360 --> 00:01:20,480
If the Soviets could put a
satellite over their heads,

31
00:01:21,120 --> 00:01:22,100
what else could they do?

32
00:01:22,750 --> 00:01:25,920
A month later, the Soviets
launched a dog named

33
00:01:25,930 --> 00:01:27,110
Laika into orbit.

34
00:01:27,670 --> 00:01:31,000
The first American attempt to
launch a satellite exploded

35
00:01:31,010 --> 00:01:33,610
on the launch pad, live on
television.

36
00:01:34,500 --> 00:01:37,943
The United States responded by
creating NASA in nineteen fifty

37
00:01:37,953 --> 00:01:38,753
eight.

38
00:01:38,917 --> 00:01:41,947
In nineteen fifty nine, NASA
picked its first

39
00:01:41,957 --> 00:01:45,297
seven astronauts, all military
test pilots.

40
00:01:46,137 --> 00:01:48,157
The press called them the
Mercury Seven.

41
00:01:49,567 --> 00:01:51,087
Then came an even bigger shock.

42
00:01:51,767 --> 00:01:54,663
On the twelfth of April,
nineteen sixty

43
00:01:54,673 --> 00:01:55,397
one,

44
00:01:55,407 --> 00:01:58,277
a twenty seven year old Soviet
pilot named Yuri

45
00:01:58,287 --> 00:02:00,987
Gagarin became the first human
in space.

46
00:02:01,917 --> 00:02:02,717
His capsule,

47
00:02:03,157 --> 00:02:05,727
Vostok One, circled the Earth
once

48
00:02:05,737 --> 00:02:07,357
in a hundred and eight minutes.

49
00:02:08,077 --> 00:02:10,297
He became the most famous person
on the planet.

50
00:02:11,167 --> 00:02:14,397
As his rocket lifted off,
Gagarin shouted a single

51
00:02:14,407 --> 00:02:14,917
word:

52
00:02:14,927 --> 00:02:16,657
poyekhali.

53
00:02:16,667 --> 00:02:17,467
Let's go.

54
00:02:18,083 --> 00:02:21,213
Behind the Soviet successes was
a brilliant engineer

55
00:02:21,223 --> 00:02:23,733
whose name was kept secret:
Sergei

56
00:02:23,743 --> 00:02:25,373
Korolev,

57
00:02:25,383 --> 00:02:27,403
known only as the Chief
Designer.

58
00:02:28,250 --> 00:02:31,320
Weeks later, Alan Shepard became
the first American

59
00:02:31,330 --> 00:02:34,680
in space, on a fifteen minute
flight that didn't even

60
00:02:34,690 --> 00:02:35,490
reach orbit.

61
00:02:36,083 --> 00:02:39,453
In February nineteen sixty two,
John Glenn became

62
00:02:39,463 --> 00:02:41,683
the first American to orbit the
Earth.

63
00:02:42,343 --> 00:02:45,753
Before he flew, he asked for one
person to double check

64
00:02:45,763 --> 00:02:48,633
the computer's numbers: the NASA
mathematician

65
00:02:48,643 --> 00:02:49,903
Katherine Johnson.

66
00:02:51,733 --> 00:02:52,773
President John F.

67
00:02:53,253 --> 00:02:55,013
Kennedy needed something bold.

68
00:02:55,553 --> 00:02:57,793
Something the Soviets could not
easily win.

69
00:02:58,500 --> 00:03:01,790
In May nineteen sixty one, he
asked Congress

70
00:03:01,800 --> 00:03:04,710
to commit America to landing a
man on the Moon,

71
00:03:04,720 --> 00:03:08,070
and returning him safely to the
Earth, before the

72
00:03:08,080 --> 00:03:08,960
decade was out.

73
00:03:09,667 --> 00:03:13,137
At the time, America had a total
of fifteen minutes

74
00:03:13,147 --> 00:03:15,387
of human spaceflight experience.

75
00:03:16,417 --> 00:03:19,747
A year later, in a speech in
Texas, he explained

76
00:03:19,757 --> 00:03:20,557
why.

77
00:03:20,777 --> 00:03:24,247
We choose to go to the Moon, he
said, not because it

78
00:03:24,257 --> 00:03:26,197
is easy, but because it is hard.

79
00:03:26,917 --> 00:03:29,947
The Apollo programme became one
of the biggest engineering

80
00:03:29,957 --> 00:03:31,497
projects in history.

81
00:03:32,017 --> 00:03:35,387
At its peak, around four hundred
thousand people worked on

82
00:03:35,397 --> 00:03:36,157
it.

83
00:03:36,167 --> 00:03:39,277
NASA flew ten crewed Gemini
missions to practise

84
00:03:39,287 --> 00:03:41,567
everything a Moon trip would
need:

85
00:03:42,327 --> 00:03:45,717
long flights, spacewalks, and
finding and

86
00:03:45,727 --> 00:03:48,207
docking with another spacecraft
in orbit.

87
00:03:48,917 --> 00:03:51,847
On Gemini Eight, a stuck
thruster sent Neil

88
00:03:51,857 --> 00:03:55,307
Armstrong's spacecraft tumbling,
once every

89
00:03:55,317 --> 00:03:55,827
second.

90
00:03:55,837 --> 00:03:57,777
He calmly regained control.

91
00:03:58,577 --> 00:03:59,517
NASA remembered.

92
00:04:00,083 --> 00:04:03,353
The plan they chose, championed
by an engineer named

93
00:04:03,363 --> 00:04:06,853
John Houbolt, was clever: leave
the main ship in

94
00:04:06,863 --> 00:04:10,163
orbit around the Moon, and send
down only a small,

95
00:04:10,603 --> 00:04:11,423
light lander.

96
00:04:12,983 --> 00:04:16,413
To get there, NASA needed the
most powerful rocket ever

97
00:04:16,423 --> 00:04:17,223
built.

98
00:04:17,303 --> 00:04:20,563
It was designed by a team led by
Wernher von Braun,

99
00:04:20,963 --> 00:04:24,093
a former Nazi rocket engineer
brought to America

100
00:04:24,103 --> 00:04:25,203
after the war.

101
00:04:25,917 --> 00:04:28,937
The Saturn Five stood a hundred
and ten metres tall,

102
00:04:29,437 --> 00:04:32,847
taller than the Statue of
Liberty, and weighed nearly
three

103
00:04:32,857 --> 00:04:34,737
thousand tonnes when fully
fuelled.

104
00:04:35,583 --> 00:04:38,733
Its five first stage engines
burned about thirteen

105
00:04:38,743 --> 00:04:41,763
tonnes of fuel and oxygen every
second.

106
00:04:42,500 --> 00:04:45,830
Thirteen Saturn Fives were
launched, and not one

107
00:04:45,840 --> 00:04:46,640
was ever lost.

108
00:04:47,417 --> 00:04:50,587
Inside the tiny command module,
three astronauts

109
00:04:50,597 --> 00:04:53,967
would ride with less room than
the inside of a

110
00:04:53,977 --> 00:04:54,797
small car.

111
00:04:55,667 --> 00:04:58,697
And the computer that guided
them had less memory than a
modern

112
00:04:58,707 --> 00:05:00,547
greetings card that plays a
tune.

113
00:05:02,233 --> 00:05:05,723
The Soviets kept scoring firsts:
the first woman

114
00:05:05,733 --> 00:05:09,223
in space, Valentina Tereshkova,
and the

115
00:05:09,233 --> 00:05:12,133
first spacewalk, by Alexei
Leonov.

116
00:05:12,833 --> 00:05:16,223
Then, in January nineteen sixty
seven,

117
00:05:16,233 --> 00:05:17,033
disaster.

118
00:05:17,393 --> 00:05:20,663
During a launch rehearsal, fire
swept through the Apollo One

119
00:05:20,673 --> 00:05:21,473
capsule.

120
00:05:21,973 --> 00:05:25,353
Astronauts Gus Grissom, Ed White
and Roger Chaffee were killed.

121
00:05:26,083 --> 00:05:28,843
NASA redesigned the capsule from
top to bottom.

122
00:05:29,243 --> 00:05:30,423
The programme went on.

123
00:05:31,167 --> 00:05:33,487
The Soviet Moon programme
struggled.

124
00:05:33,927 --> 00:05:37,183
Korolev had died during surgery
in nineteen

125
00:05:37,193 --> 00:05:40,577
sixty six, and their giant N1
rocket exploded

126
00:05:40,587 --> 00:05:42,787
on every one of its four
launches.

127
00:05:44,567 --> 00:05:48,057
In December nineteen sixty
eight, Apollo Eight carried

128
00:05:48,067 --> 00:05:50,817
three astronauts all the way to
the Moon,

129
00:05:50,827 --> 00:05:52,607
and around it, ten times.

130
00:05:53,500 --> 00:05:56,970
On Christmas Eve, as their
spacecraft came around from the

131
00:05:56,980 --> 00:06:00,270
far side, they saw the Earth
rising over the lunar

132
00:06:00,280 --> 00:06:00,850
horizon,

133
00:06:00,860 --> 00:06:02,180
and grabbed a camera.

134
00:06:02,917 --> 00:06:06,027
That photograph, Earthrise,
showed our whole

135
00:06:06,037 --> 00:06:09,387
world as a small, fragile blue
marble

136
00:06:09,397 --> 00:06:10,197
in the darkness.

137
00:06:11,167 --> 00:06:14,397
In May nineteen sixty nine,
Apollo Ten flew

138
00:06:14,407 --> 00:06:17,817
a full dress rehearsal, taking
the lunar module to

139
00:06:17,827 --> 00:06:20,867
within about fifteen kilometres
of the Moon's surface.

140
00:06:21,867 --> 00:06:22,667
Everything was ready.

141
00:06:23,983 --> 00:06:27,313
On the sixteenth of July,
nineteen sixty nine,

142
00:06:27,323 --> 00:06:28,123
Neil Armstrong,

143
00:06:28,683 --> 00:06:32,103
Buzz Aldrin and Michael Collins
launched on Apollo Eleven.

144
00:06:32,917 --> 00:06:36,107
Around a million people gathered
near Cape Kennedy to watch

145
00:06:36,117 --> 00:06:36,917
the launch.

146
00:06:37,583 --> 00:06:41,073
Four days later, Armstrong and
Aldrin climbed into the

147
00:06:41,083 --> 00:06:42,883
lunar module, named Eagle,

148
00:06:43,523 --> 00:06:44,543
and began their descent.

149
00:06:45,363 --> 00:06:46,843
Collins stayed behind in orbit.

150
00:06:47,667 --> 00:06:49,847
Each time Collins passed behind
the Moon,

151
00:06:50,567 --> 00:06:53,927
he lost all radio contact with
Earth for about forty

152
00:06:53,937 --> 00:06:55,007
eight minutes.

153
00:06:55,667 --> 00:06:58,607
No human had ever been so far
from everyone else.

154
00:06:59,500 --> 00:07:02,950
On the way down, the guidance
computer flashed alarms they

155
00:07:02,960 --> 00:07:04,120
had never seen before,

156
00:07:04,840 --> 00:07:07,970
codes twelve oh two and twelve
oh one.

157
00:07:07,980 --> 00:07:08,820
It was overloaded.

158
00:07:09,700 --> 00:07:11,700
Mission control decided to carry
on.

159
00:07:12,417 --> 00:07:15,907
Then Armstrong saw that the
computer was steering them into

160
00:07:15,917 --> 00:07:17,457
a crater full of boulders.

161
00:07:17,897 --> 00:07:20,987
He took manual control, and flew
on, searching

162
00:07:20,997 --> 00:07:22,137
for a safe spot.

163
00:07:22,917 --> 00:07:26,087
They touched down with less than
a minute of fuel left to

164
00:07:26,097 --> 00:07:26,897
spare.

165
00:07:27,377 --> 00:07:30,737
Houston, Armstrong radioed,
Tranquility Base here.

166
00:07:31,197 --> 00:07:32,157
The Eagle has landed.

167
00:07:33,000 --> 00:07:36,250
Six and a half hours later,
Armstrong stepped

168
00:07:36,260 --> 00:07:37,140
onto the surface.

169
00:07:37,840 --> 00:07:40,360
That's one small step for man,
he said,

170
00:07:41,040 --> 00:07:42,300
one giant leap for mankind.

171
00:07:43,417 --> 00:07:44,557
Aldrin joined him.

172
00:07:45,097 --> 00:07:48,427
They planted a flag, collected
rocks, and set up

173
00:07:48,437 --> 00:07:49,237
experiments,

174
00:07:49,557 --> 00:07:52,157
some of which are still being
used today.

175
00:07:52,833 --> 00:07:55,783
They spent about two and a half
hours outside,

176
00:07:55,793 --> 00:07:58,893
and gathered more than twenty
kilogrammes of Moon rock.

177
00:07:59,393 --> 00:08:02,823
They left behind a plaque that
reads: we came in peace for

178
00:08:02,833 --> 00:08:03,633
all mankind.

179
00:08:04,500 --> 00:08:06,880
When Eagle blasted off to rejoin
Collins,

180
00:08:07,420 --> 00:08:10,020
the exhaust knocked over the
flag they had planted.

181
00:08:10,917 --> 00:08:14,187
On the twenty fourth of July,
the crew splashed down

182
00:08:14,197 --> 00:08:15,397
in the Pacific Ocean.

183
00:08:15,977 --> 00:08:18,917
Just in case they had brought
back Moon germs,

184
00:08:19,097 --> 00:08:21,417
they were kept in quarantine for
three weeks.

185
00:08:22,333 --> 00:08:25,673
Back on Earth, people wept and
cheered in the streets.

186
00:08:27,567 --> 00:08:30,727
Between nineteen sixty nine and
nineteen seventy

187
00:08:30,737 --> 00:08:31,222
two,

188
00:08:31,232 --> 00:08:33,487
twelve men walked on the Moon.

189
00:08:34,067 --> 00:08:36,107
Since then, no one has gone
back.

190
00:08:36,833 --> 00:08:39,723
In nineteen seventy, an
explosion crippled Apollo

191
00:08:39,733 --> 00:08:42,233
Thirteen on its way to the Moon.

192
00:08:42,833 --> 00:08:46,123
With mission control's help, the
crew used the lunar module

193
00:08:46,133 --> 00:08:48,313
as a lifeboat, and made it home.

194
00:08:49,083 --> 00:08:51,203
The last astronaut to walk on
the Moon,

195
00:08:51,783 --> 00:08:54,867
Gene Cernan, left in December
nineteen seventy

196
00:08:54,877 --> 00:08:55,513
two.

197
00:08:55,523 --> 00:08:58,833
The Apollo programme had cost
around twenty five billion
dollars

198
00:08:58,843 --> 00:09:00,123
at the time.

199
00:09:00,833 --> 00:09:02,733
The race left behind more than
footprints.

200
00:09:03,633 --> 00:09:04,873
It pushed forward computers,

201
00:09:05,493 --> 00:09:06,293
materials,

202
00:09:06,653 --> 00:09:09,983
weather satellites and medicine,
and inspired a generation

203
00:09:09,993 --> 00:09:12,493
to become scientists and
engineers.

204
00:09:14,483 --> 00:09:16,623
And it gave us that picture of
Earthrise:

205
00:09:17,203 --> 00:09:20,083
a reminder that we all share one
small planet.
